-- Lesson react-native-basics/2/4: Handling touches
update public.lesson_contents set body = 'By the end of this lesson, you will be able to capture tap and press interactions in your app using the standard `Button` component, classic `Touchable` wrappers, and the modern `Pressable` component.

## The standard Button component

React Native provides a basic `Button` component that renders nicely across platforms. On iOS, it displays as coloured label text resembling a standard system button. On Android, it renders as a rounded rectangle with light text.

A minimal `Button` example includes `title` for the button label and `onPress` for the function to execute when tapped. You can also specify an optional `color` prop to change the colour of the button.

```tsx
import type { JSX } from ''react'';
import { Button, StyleSheet, View } from ''react-native'';

export default function App(): JSX.Element {
  return (
    <View style={styles.container}>
      <Button
        title="Save Changes"
        color="#841584"
        onPress={(): void => console.log(''Button pressed'')}
      />
    </View>
  );
}

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: {
    flex: 1,
    justifyContent: ''center'',
    padding: 20,
  },
});
```

While `Button` is quick to display, it does not provide custom styling options. For bespoke designs, React Native provides dedicated touchable wrappers.

## Classic Touchable components

When a basic button does not look right for your app, you can build your own using touchable components. These components do not provide default styling, but they capture tap gestures and display visual feedback:

- `TouchableOpacity`: Reduces the opacity of the button, allowing the background to show through while pressing down.
- `TouchableHighlight`: Darkens the view''s background when pressed down, suitable for use anywhere you would use a button or link on the web.
- `TouchableNativeFeedback`: Displays ink surface reaction ripples on Android in response to touch. This component is specific to Android.
- `TouchableWithoutFeedback`: Handles tap gestures without displaying any visual feedback.

All touchable components accept an `onPress` callback. To detect when someone holds down on an element for a set amount of time, you can pass a function to the `onLongPress` prop.

## Building interactive elements with Pressable

The `Pressable` component is a core wrapper capable of detecting distinct stages of press interactions on any of its children. When a press begins, `onPressIn` is called when activated. Following `onPressIn`, one of two outcomes occurs:

1. The user removes their finger, triggering `onPressOut` followed by `onPress`.
2. The user leaves their finger down longer than 500 milliseconds (customisable using `delayLongPress`), which triggers `onLongPress`. When they eventually remove their finger, `onPressOut` still fires.

A powerful feature of `Pressable` is that both its `style` and `children` props can accept a function receiving an object with a `pressed` boolean. This lets you reactively change appearance without managing manual state.

```tsx
import type { JSX } from ''react'';
import { Pressable, StyleSheet, Text } from ''react-native'';

export default function ActionCard(): JSX.Element {
  return (
    <Pressable
      onPress={(): void => console.log(''Tapped card'')}
      style={({ pressed }: { pressed: boolean }) => [
        styles.card,
        { backgroundColor: pressed ? ''#d2e6ff'' : ''#ffffff'' },
      ]}
      hitSlop={10}>
      {({ pressed }: { pressed: boolean }): JSX.Element => (
        <Text style={styles.text}>{pressed ? ''Pressed!'' : ''Tap me''}</Text>
      )}
    </Pressable>
  );
}

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  card: {
    padding: 16,
    borderRadius: 8,
    borderWidth: 1,
    borderColor: ''#cccccc'',
  },
  text: { fontSize: 16 },
});
```

`Pressable` also supports `hitSlop`, which sets additional distance outside of the element where a press can be detected. Note that touch areas never extend past parent view bounds.

### Try it

Create a custom button using `Pressable`. Give it a default background colour, and use the `style` function to change that colour whenever `pressed` is true. Add an `onLongPress` prop that logs a message to the console after holding down on the button.

### Key points

- `Button` provides a basic, platform-adapted component using `title`, `color`, and `onPress`.
- `TouchableOpacity` and `TouchableHighlight` provide ready-made visual feedback effects for custom layouts.
- `Pressable` tracks touch states including `onPressIn`, `onPressOut`, `onPress`, and `onLongPress`.
- `Pressable` accepts a function for `style` and `children` that receives a `pressed` status flag.
- `hitSlop` extends the interactive press detection area beyond the visual bounds of an element.' || coalesce(substring(body from E'\n\n\\*Video: [^\n]*\\*$'), '') where lesson_id = md5('lesson:react-native-basics:2:4')::uuid;
