-- Lesson react-native-basics/3/2: Passing data between screens
update public.lesson_contents set body = 'In this lesson, you will learn how to pass data between screens in Expo Router and retrieve that information on destination screens using route parameters.

## Sending parameters to a screen

Expo Router uses URL patterns to share data across screens. You can send data by building dynamic route paths, appending URL query parameters, or passing an object into the `Link` component or the `router.navigate` function. Dynamic routes can be linked directly with their full URL string or by supplying a structured navigation object.

When using an object, specify the destination `pathname` alongside a `params` object containing key-value pairs:

```tsx
import { View, Pressable, Text } from ''react-native'';
import { Link, useRouter } from ''expo-router'';

export default function HomeScreen() {
  const router = useRouter();

  return (
    <View>
      <Link href={{ pathname: ''/user/[id]'', params: { id: ''bacon'' } }}>
        View user Bacon
      </Link>
      <Pressable
        onPress={() =>
          router.navigate({ pathname: ''/user/[id]'', params: { id: ''bacon'' } })
        }
      >
        <Text>Go to user imperatively</Text>
      </Pressable>
    </View>
  );
}
```

You can also supply query parameters directly inside a string URL, such as `href="/users?limit=20"`, or as properties inside the `params` object. Any parameter that does not match a dynamic segment variable name is treated as a query parameter.

## Reading parameters with useLocalSearchParams

To access parameters inside the destination screen, use the `useLocalSearchParams` hook. In this course, examples use TypeScript, so you can provide a type definition for the parameters returned by the hook. This ensures that route parameters have predictable types throughout your component code:

```tsx
import { View, Text } from ''react-native'';
import { useLocalSearchParams } from ''expo-router'';

type UserSearchParams = {
  id: string;
  limit?: string;
};

export default function UserScreen() {
  const { id, limit } = useLocalSearchParams<UserSearchParams>();

  return (
    <View>
      <Text>User ID: {id}</Text>
      <Text>Limit: {limit}</Text>
    </View>
  );
}
```

The hook extracts both dynamic route variables (such as an `id` defined by a `[id].tsx` file) and optional query values (such as `limit`).

## Updating parameters without navigating

Query parameters can be updated without navigating to a new page. Expo Router allows you to update parameters either imperatively or declaratively.

Imperative updates use the `router.setParams` method provided by the `useRouter` hook:

```tsx
import { Pressable, Text } from ''react-native'';
import { useRouter } from ''expo-router'';

export default function UpdateLimit() {
  const router = useRouter();

  return (
    <Pressable onPress={() => router.setParams({ limit: 50 })}>
      <Text>View more users</Text>
    </Pressable>
  );
}
```

You can achieve the same behaviour declaratively by rendering a `Link` component that points to the same URL as the current page with updated query parameters:

```tsx
import { Link } from ''expo-router'';

export default function UpdateLink() {
  return <Link href="/users?limit=50">View more users</Link>;
}
```

### Try it

Create a component with a `Link` that navigates to a `/users` path with a query parameter of `?limit=10`. Then, set up the receiving screen to read `limit` using `useLocalSearchParams` and display it in a `Text` element.

### Key points

- Parameters can be passed as query strings or within a `params` object using `Link` or `router.navigate`.
- Any key in `params` that does not match a dynamic segment name becomes a query parameter.
- The `useLocalSearchParams` hook reads both dynamic segment variables and query parameters on the target screen.
- Query parameters on the active screen can be updated in place using `router.setParams` or a `Link` with updated parameters.' || coalesce(substring(body from E'\n\n\\*Video: [^\n]*\\*$'), '') where lesson_id = md5('lesson:react-native-basics:3:2')::uuid;
delete from public.lesson_sources where lesson_id = md5('lesson:react-native-basics:3:2')::uuid;
