  (md5('lesson:ui-design-fundamentals:1:1')::uuid, 2, 'Design principles (U.S. Web Design System)', 'U.S. General Services Administration (public domain)', 'https://designsystem.digital.gov/design-principles/', 'CC0 1.0 (US public domain)', 'https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/', null, 'adapted');

-- Lesson ui-design-fundamentals/1/2: Hierarchy and focus
update public.lesson_contents set body = 'In this lesson, you will learn how to organise an interface so that every user can understand its structure and navigate it smoothly. You will structure content using semantic landmarks and headings, manage logical focus order for keyboard navigation, and create clear, high-contrast focus indicators.

## Establishing hierarchy with landmarks and headings

Visual styling alone is not enough to communicate the hierarchy of an interface. Using semantic structural elements provides an outline of your content, creating anchor points that assistive technologies such as screen readers rely on for navigation.

Landmarks group large regions of a page into recognised areas:

- `<header>` identifies introductory banner content.
- `<nav>` contains navigational links.
- `<main>` holds the primary content of the page.
- `<footer>` marks concluding information, such as copyright notices.
- `<section>` groups related content, and needs an accessible name (such as an `aria-label`) to expose its region role.

Headings reinforce this outline by ranking content from most important (`<h1>`) down to least important (`<h6>`). Always keep headings in sequential order without skipping levels (for example, do not jump straight from an `<h1>` to an `<h5>`). If you need a smaller heading to appear larger visually, decouple your CSS styles from your heading level rather than picking an inaccurate heading tag:

```html
<header>
  <h1>Stamp collecting</h1>
  <nav>
    <a href="#main-content">Skip to main content</a>
  </nav>
</header>
<main id="main-content">
  <section aria-label="Introduction to stamp collecting">
    <h2>Equipment you will need</h2>
    <p>A stockbook, magnifying glass, and stamp tongs.</p>
  </section>
</main>
```

## Managing logical focus and navigation order

Focus refers to which interactive element on the screen currently receives input from a keyboard or switch device. The navigation or tab order must follow a logical path that matches the visual layout, moving from top to bottom and left to right for most languages.

Native interactive elements—such as links, form inputs, and buttons—are naturally focusable and included in the tab order by default. When managing focus manually:

- Use `tabindex="0"` to place a non-interactive element into the natural focus order according to its position in the DOM.
- Use `tabindex="-1"` to remove an inactive element (such as an inoperative button) from the natural focus order while keeping it script-focusable.
- Avoid positive numbers (like `tabindex="1"` or `tabindex="2"`), as these override the document flow and lead to confusing jumps across the screen.

For pages with repetitive header menus, include a skip link as the first focusable element. A link reading "Skip to main content" allows keyboard users to bypass redundant navigation bars and jump straight to the `<main>` area.

## Designing visible focus indicators

A visible focus indicator lets sighted keyboard users identify exactly where they are on the page at any moment. Browsers provide default focus rings, but stylesheets frequently strip them away using `outline: none` or `outline: 0`. Never remove focus outlines without supplying an accessible replacement.

When styling custom focus states:

- Maintain at least a 3:1 colour contrast ratio for all focus indicators against the background.
- Avoid relying on colour alone; use distinct outlines, borders, underlines, or background shifts.
- You can tailor focus styles by element type. For example, links can display a yellow background with a black bottom border, whereas form inputs receive a yellow outline alongside a thick black border so they stand out against both light and dark backgrounds.

To ensure focus indicators remain visible rather than being stripped away, you can explicitly define outline properties in CSS:

```css
a:focus {
  outline: auto 5px Highlight;
  outline: auto 5px -webkit-focus-ring-color;
}
```

### Try it

Take a web page or template you have built. Use your keyboard''s Tab key to move through the page from top to bottom. Check whether the focus indicator remains visible on every link and form field, verify that the order matches the visual reading flow, and ensure no positive `tabindex` attributes are present.

### Key points

- Semantic landmarks like `<header>`, `<main>`, and `<footer>` define page regions, while heading levels (`<h1>` to `<h6>`) must follow an unbroken sequence.
- Focus order should naturally mirror the visual flow of the page, moving top to bottom without using positive `tabindex` values.
- Skip links allow keyboard users to bypass repetitive header navigation and jump directly to primary content.
- Focus indicators must remain clearly visible at all times, maintaining at least a 3:1 contrast ratio against the background.' || coalesce(substring(body from E'\n\n\\*Video: [^\n]*\\*$'), '') where lesson_id = md5('lesson:ui-design-fundamentals:1:2')::uuid;
