-- Lesson ui-design-fundamentals/1/3: Learning from great apps
update public.lesson_contents set body = 'In this lesson, you will learn how to analyse and apply proven interface patterns from great applications. You will explore how responsive interfaces adapt across screen sizes, how to structure navigation and overflow content, and how to tailor interactions to different input devices.

## Responsive patterns over simple scaling

When studying well-crafted digital products, one clear rule emerges: a small screen should not simply be a shrunk-down version of a desktop interface, and a large display should not merely be a blown-up mobile screen. Great apps adapt their interface elements to make the best use of each form factor.

Designing for smaller screens first ensures that your mobile experience feels natural rather than like an afterthought. When adapting layouts, you might need to apply very different styles to the exact same content to suit different viewports.

## Navigation and progressive disclosure

Examining navigation in successful apps reveals how designers handle limited screen space. Hiding navigation behind a button forces people to take two steps just to browse: opening the menu, then selecting a destination. Until they interact with the control, users are left wondering where they can go.

Great designs use balanced strategies:

- **Visible navigation**: When you have a small number of links, keep them visible even on compact screens.
- **The overflow pattern**: When dealing with many links, keep them on a single line and allow horizontal swiping across the screen edge. Adding a visual cue, such as a subtle gradient or shadow over the truncated edge, indicates that more items exist. Always place the most critical links first.
- **Progressive disclosure**: If space constraints force you to hide links behind a toggle as a last resort, ensure the toggle button has a clear text label. Relying solely on unlabelled icons—such as three horizontal lines or stacked dots—creates "mystery meat" navigation, where users cannot tell what a button does until they tap it.

## Adapting content with carousels and grids

Carousels frequently solve organisational debates rather than user needs, as users often miss content tucked off-screen. However, thoughtful applications use hybrid layouts: displaying items in a swipeable row on compact screens, and expanding them into a multi-column grid on wider screens.

CSS scroll snap properties allow you to create smooth swipeable containers:

```css
@media (max-width: 50em) {
  .cards {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    overflow-x: auto;
    scroll-snap-type: inline mandatory;
    scroll-behavior: smooth;
  }
  .cards .card {
    flex-shrink: 0;
    flex-basis: 15em;
    scroll-snap-align: start;
  }
}
```

Crucially, items in an overflow carousel should not fill the entire viewport width. Allowing the next card to peek in from the edge visually signals that there is more content to explore.

## Respecting inputs and interaction

Great interfaces also adapt to how people interact with devices. Equating small screens strictly with touch and large screens with a mouse is inaccurate, as many laptops have touchscreens and tablets can use trackpads. Instead, you can inspect input mechanisms using media queries.

The `pointer: coarse` query identifies less accurate inputs, such as a finger on a touchscreen, allowing you to enlarge interactive elements:

```css
button {
  padding: 0.5em 1em;
}

@media (pointer: coarse) {
  button {
    padding: 1em 2em;
  }
}
```

According to Fitts''s Law, larger target areas require less concentration and benefit everyone, so avoid shrinking buttons for fine pointers. In addition, streamline virtual keyboard experiences on touchscreen devices by using descriptive form attributes, such as `type="email"`, `inputmode="numeric"` for whole numbers, and appropriate `autocomplete` values.

### Try it

Open a favourite website on both a phone and a computer. Compare how the primary navigation behaves. Does the mobile view keep links visible, employ an overflow scroller, or tuck items behind a labelled toggle? Notice whether buttons and form inputs adapt to your fingers.

### Key points

- Responsive designs adapt interface elements to each screen size rather than merely shrinking or blowing up layouts.
- Avoid hiding navigation whenever possible; use visible links, horizontal overflow scrolling, or clearly labelled toggles.
- Avoid unlabelled icon buttons to prevent mystery meat navigation.
- Enlarge interactive targets for coarse pointers, remembering that generous tap targets benefit all users.
- Enhance mobile data entry by matching virtual keyboards to inputs with `type`, `inputmode`, and `autocomplete`.' || coalesce(substring(body from E'\n\n\\*Video: [^\n]*\\*$'), '') where lesson_id = md5('lesson:ui-design-fundamentals:1:3')::uuid;
