-- Lesson ui-design-fundamentals/2/1: Choosing a typeface
update public.lesson_contents set body = 'In this lesson, you will learn how to select typefaces for your user interfaces, balance visual expression with load performance, and implement fonts cleanly in your stylesheets.

## System fonts and web fonts

A typeface acts as a voice for your written words. In the early days of the web, designers were limited to system fonts already installed on a user''s device. Today, web fonts let you select typefaces that match the specific tone and character of your product.

However, some projects have strict rules about font choices. For example, the GOV.UK Design System requires services hosted on the `service.gov.uk` subdomain to use the GDS Transport font, while services hosted outside that domain must use an alternative typeface. When choosing a typeface, you must consider whether you are bound by existing organisation guidelines or free to introduce a new web font.

While web fonts offer creative freedom, system fonts remain extremely fast because they require no network downloads. Using system fonts (such as referencing `system-ui`) provides immediate rendering and can even grant the benefits of modern font features without downloading any extra assets.

## Defining web fonts with `@font-face`

When you decide to use a custom web font, you inform the browser where to find the font files using the `@font-face` CSS rule. For web delivery, modern browsers support the `woff2` format, which provides the best compression and performance gains.

```css
@font-face {
  font-family: Roboto;
  src: url(''/fonts/roboto-regular.woff2'') format(''woff2'');
}

body {
  font-family: Roboto, sans-serif;
}
```

Always specify a fallback system font or generic family (such as `sans-serif`) after your custom typeface. If the network is slow or the file fails to arrive, the browser can still display the content clearly using the fallback.

## Balancing loading and performance

Every font file you load adds weight to your page and can increase load times. Because an interface that feels fast is essential to a good user experience, you must manage how font assets load:

- **Preload critical fonts**: You can ask browsers to prioritise font downloads by adding a `<link>` tag in your HTML head with `rel="preload"`, `as="font"`, and `crossorigin`.
- **Control the display swap**: Use the `font-display` descriptor inside your `@font-face` rule to choose how text behaves while downloading. A value of `swap` shows fallback system text first and swaps in the web font once loaded. A value of `fallback` gives the web font a brief time to load before committing to the system font for the remainder of the visit.

```css
@font-face {
  font-family: Roboto;
  src: url(''/fonts/roboto-regular.woff2'') format(''woff2'');
  font-display: swap;
}

body {
  font-family: Roboto, sans-serif;
}
```

## Using variable fonts

Traditional typefaces require a separate file for every weight and style you need, such as regular, bold, and extra bold. If you use several variations, those files accumulate and slow down your application.

Variable fonts solve this problem. A single variable font file contains all the design data needed to generate a continuous spectrum of weights and styles. Although one variable font file is slightly larger than a single standard weight file, it is typically much smaller than downloading multiple distinct weight files. Choosing a variable font gives you wide typographic flexibility while keeping file transfers small.

### Try it

Write a CSS snippet that registers a local web font called `OpenSans` using the `woff2` format, configures `font-display: swap` inside the `@font-face` rule, and applies it to your `body` with a `sans-serif` fallback.

### Key points

- Typefaces set the voice for your interface, but you must balance visual character against download speed.
- Use `woff2` as your primary font format because it offers the strongest compression and browser support.
- Manage font rendering using `font-display` within `@font-face` to avoid leaving users with blank screens while fonts download.
- Variable fonts allow multiple weights and styles within a single file, significantly reducing network requests.' || coalesce(substring(body from E'\n\n\\*Video: [^\n]*\\*$'), '') where lesson_id = md5('lesson:ui-design-fundamentals:2:1')::uuid;
delete from public.lesson_sources where lesson_id = md5('lesson:ui-design-fundamentals:2:1')::uuid;
