-- Lesson ui-design-fundamentals/3/1: Grids without the pain
update public.lesson_contents set body = 'You will be able to plan high-level page layouts, divide space into balanced columns, and build fluid layouts that adapt to any screen size without writing complex rules.

## Start with a single column

When organising the high-level structure of a page—known as a macro layout—always design for smaller screens first. Before adding any multi-column layout styles, ensure that your content flows logically in a default single-column order. 

Starting with a single column guarantees that mobile devices display content cleanly. When screens become wider, placing long blocks of text across the full width makes lines difficult to read. As a rule of thumb, you should aim for no more than 75 characters per line. Setting your main content to take up roughly two-thirds of the container width on desktop helps prevent line lengths from becoming too wide.

## Defining columns with CSS grid

When sufficient screen width is available, you can expand a single-column layout into multiple columns. While you can achieve column layouts using either flexbox or CSS grid, grid is often much simpler for page-level structures.

With flexbox, you must define the sizing rules on both the parent container and each individual child element. With grid, you can encapsulate the entire column structure on the parent container in a single declaration:

```css
@media (min-width: 45em) {
  main {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 2fr 1fr;
  }
}
```

In this rule, the primary content area automatically receives two-thirds of the available space, while the secondary area receives one-third. 

## Responsive grids without media queries

Media queries are helpful for switching macro layouts at specific breakpoints, such as shifting from one column to two. However, using media queries to arrange collections of components—such as a list of cards—can quickly become tedious to maintain as you add more breakpoints.

Instead of managing multiple breakpoints, you can instruct the browser to calculate the layout automatically using `repeat()`, `auto-fill`, and `minmax()`:

```css
.cards {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(15em, 1fr));
  grid-gap: 1em;
}
```

This declaration tells the container to fit as many cards onto a line as will fit, with each card having a minimum width of `15em` and expanding equally with `1fr` to fill remaining space. If you attempt this pattern with flexbox, items on a partially filled final row stretch across the entire width. CSS grid preserves the alignment of both rows and columns.

### Try it

Create a container class named `.card-grid` that displays child cards in a grid. Set `grid-gap` to `1em`, and configure `grid-template-columns` so that cards have a minimum width of `15em` and automatically fill each row while maintaining aligned columns.

### Key points

- Start layouts with a single-column structure for small screens and design for screen sizes rather than specific devices.
- Restrict line lengths for reading comfort, using common layouts like two-thirds width for main content.
- CSS grid encapsulates macro layout rules on the parent container rather than requiring separate rules on every child element.
- Use `repeat(auto-fill, minmax(...))` to create fluid, multi-column card layouts without writing multiple media queries.' || coalesce(substring(body from E'\n\n\\*Video: [^\n]*\\*$'), '') where lesson_id = md5('lesson:ui-design-fundamentals:3:1')::uuid;
delete from public.lesson_sources where lesson_id = md5('lesson:ui-design-fundamentals:3:1')::uuid;
insert into public.lesson_sources (lesson_id, position, title, author, url, licence, licence_url, attribution_note, role) values
  (md5('lesson:ui-design-fundamentals:3:1')::uuid, 1, 'Macro layouts (web.dev Learn Design and Learn Accessibility)', 'Google LLC and web.dev contributors', 'https://web.dev/learn/design/macro-layouts', 'CC BY 3.0', 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/', null, 'adapted'),
  (md5('lesson:ui-design-fundamentals:3:1')::uuid, 2, 'Layout (GOV.UK Design System)', '© Crown copyright (Government Digital Service)', 'https://design-system.service.gov.uk/styles/layout/', 'MIT (repository) and OGL v3.0 (site content)', 'https://opensource.org/license/mit', 'Contains public sector information licensed under the Open Government Licence v3.0.', 'adapted');

-- Lesson ui-design-fundamentals/3/2: Spacing systems
update public.lesson_contents set body = 'In UI design, setting margins and padding with random pixel values leads to messy, inconsistent layouts. In this lesson, you will learn how to organise spacing using structured spacing units, distinguish between static and responsive spacing scales, and apply spacing tokens cleanly across your designs.

## Spacing units and scales

Rather than picking arbitrary pixel numbers for every element, design systems define a restricted set of spacing tokens. For example, the U.S. Web Design System (USWDS) builds its unit tokens primarily on multiples of 8px. Smaller units can represent finer increments, such as half-steps or pixel values, while larger spacing scales upward predictably.

Other systems, such as the GOV.UK Design System and the NHS digital service manual, index their spacing scales using numeric units from 0 to 9. Unit 0 removes spacing entirely (0px). In the GOV.UK static scale, units 1 to 6 increase in 5px increments (5px up to 30px), while units 7, 8, and 9 scale in 10px increments (40px, 50px, and 60px). In the NHS system, units use steps such as 4px, 8px, 16px, 24px, and beyond. Restricting your choices to a defined scale ensures visual rhythm across the whole interface and prevents slight visual discrepancies between adjacent components.

## Static and responsive spacing

When designing for different viewports, spacing needs can differ between handheld devices and large monitors:

- **Static spacing**: Keeps the exact same margin or padding across all screen sizes. This is ideal for internal component spacing that should not stretch or shrink, such as the distance between an icon and its label.
- **Responsive spacing**: Adapts automatically based on the device width. For instance, the GOV.UK Design System uses a tablet breakpoint at 640px, while the NHS design system switches to large-screen spacing above 768px. Below the breakpoint, generous margins shrink down so content fits smaller screens without feeling cramped.

Notably, both systems keep the smallest units (units 0 to 3 in GOV.UK, and units 0 to 2 in NHS) identical across all screen sizes. Tiny gaps inside compact components do not need to shrink on mobile devices, whereas large gaps between sections benefit from tightening on narrow viewports.

## Applying spacing in code

Design systems provide consistent ways to apply these spacing tokens in stylesheets and markup, avoiding hard-coded pixel values:

1. **Utility classes**: Class names let you add spacing directly to markup. In the NHS system, classes start with `nhsuk-u-margin-` or `nhsuk-u-padding-`, followed by the unit number (such as `nhsuk-u-margin-9` or `nhsuk-u-padding-right-5`). GOV.UK uses classes starting with `govuk-!-margin-` or `govuk-!-padding-`. USWDS provides utility classes such as `.padding-x-2` or `.margin-x-neg-2`.
2. **Helper functions and mixins**: When writing component stylesheets, design systems supply preprocessor functions and mixins to read token values. For example, GOV.UK provides `govuk-spacing(6)` for static spacing and mixins like `@include govuk-responsive-margin(6, "bottom");` to automate viewport adjustments. In standard CSS without a preprocessor, these correspond to static declarations and media query overrides.
3. **Negative spacing**: When elements need to overlap or pull backwards, spacing systems support negative units. Functions like `govuk-spacing(-3)` or USWDS utility tokens like `neg-2` allow you to apply negative margins systematically.

```css
/* Responsive unit 6 padding and static unit 4 margin in CSS */
.card {
  padding: 20px;
  margin-bottom: 20px;
}

@media (min-width: 640px) {
  .card {
    padding: 30px;
  }
}

.card__header {
  margin-top: -10px;
}
```

### Try it

Review a layout with a header, a content card, and a button. Decide which spacing tokens should be static (such as a 10px gap between an icon and button text) and which should be responsive (such as a 40px outer page margin that shrinks to 25px on small screens in GOV.UK unit 7). Write down the utility class names or mixin calls you would use to apply them.

### Key points

- Spacing systems replace arbitrary pixel choices with a predictable scale, such as multiples of 8px or indexed units from 0 to 9.
- Static spacing remains unchanged across all screen sizes, while responsive spacing adjusts above breakpoints (such as 640px or 768px).
- Small spacing units remain constant across viewports to preserve internal component alignment.
- Systems provide utility classes, functions, and mixins that support directional overrides and negative values.' || coalesce(substring(body from E'\n\n\\*Video: [^\n]*\\*$'), '') where lesson_id = md5('lesson:ui-design-fundamentals:3:2')::uuid;
