-- Lesson ui-design-fundamentals/3/3: Designing a real screen
update public.lesson_contents set body = 'When you build a real screen, you bring together macro page structures and micro component layouts. In this lesson, you will learn how to structure a full page from top to bottom and arrange self-contained components within it.

## Structuring the macro page

A full screen needs a clear hierarchy to keep pages consistent and manageable. At the page level, structure is broken down into distinct functional areas:

- **Skip link**: Rendered before the `<header>` element.
- **Header**: Contains branding, an optional service name, and navigation.
- **Width container**: Wraps content to limit the width of the page and holds items like back links or breadcrumbs.
- **Main content**: The primary `<main>` element where your core page content lives.
- **Footer**: Sits at the bottom of the template as a dedicated `<footer>` element.

Wrapping content in a width container limits the width of the page across viewports.

When a layout instead structures page-level columns directly inside the `<body>` element—such as a `<body>` containing only `<main>` and `<aside>` elements—you can use a media query to create a two-column grid when space permits:

```css
@media (min-width: 45em) {
  body {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 3fr 1fr;
  }
}
```

## Component micro-layouts

Page layouts handle the screen as a whole, but smaller elements inside the page require their own micro-layouts. A media card, a list item, or a section heading should organise its own internal elements without relying on the broader page structure.

Flexbox is ideal for micro-layouts because it allows you to design from the content out. You can define how elements grow or shrink relative to one another while placing constraints on fixed assets:

```css
.media {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 1em;
}

.media-illustration {
  flex: 1;
  max-inline-size: 200px;
}

.media-content {
  flex: 3;
}
```

In this component, the illustration takes up one quarter of the available space and the text takes up three quarters, while the image is constrained so that it never exceeds 200 pixels.

## Designing components for any context

A resilient screen design ensures components look balanced whether placed in a wide main area or a narrow sidebar. If a component relies only on viewport width, it cannot know whether it is inside a tight column or a spacious banner.

By using container queries, you declare parent elements as containers to query their inline dimension:

```css
main,
aside {
  container-type: inline-size;
}

.media-illustration {
  max-width: 200px;
  margin: auto;
}

@container (min-width: 25em) {
  .media {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 1em;
  }

  .media-illustration {
    flex: 1;
  }

  .media-content {
    flex: 3;
  }
}
```

Setting base styles on `.media-illustration` centres the image and restricts its maximum width. When a media object is inside a container narrower than `25em`, the flexbox styles are not applied, so the image and content appear ordered vertically.

When the containing element is wider than `25em`, the container query applies the flexbox layout alongside the flex item sizing (`flex: 1` and `flex: 3`), displaying the image and text side by side. Combining page-level media queries for macro columns with container-aware rules for micro-layouts ensures components adapt cleanly anywhere on the screen.

### Try it

1. Define a page layout that places a `main` column and an `aside` sidebar side by side on wide viewports using CSS grid.
2. Set `container-type: inline-size` on both the `main` and `aside` elements.
3. Add a card component with stacked base styles and a container query at `25em` so it stacks vertically in the narrow sidebar but lays out side by side in the main column.

### Key points

- Page templates divide screens into predictable areas, including a skip link rendered before the header, a header, a width container, main content, and a footer.
- Width containers limit the width of the page and can house items such as back links or breadcrumbs.
- Micro-layouts manage the internal spacing and sizing of individual components using flexbox or CSS grid.
- Combining media queries for macro page columns with container-aware rules for micro-layouts ensures components adapt cleanly anywhere on the screen.' || coalesce(substring(body from E'\n\n\\*Video: [^\n]*\\*$'), '') where lesson_id = md5('lesson:ui-design-fundamentals:3:3')::uuid;
delete from public.lesson_sources where lesson_id = md5('lesson:ui-design-fundamentals:3:3')::uuid;
